          "derivation": "xojaste خجسته 'blessed, auspicious' is a straightforward and well-attested descendant: Av. *hu-ǰasta- 'well-wished-for' > MP huǰastag > NP xojaste, with hu- > xo- (the regular Persian treatment of Old Iranian hu- 'good', as in xoš, xor-) and loss of the MP -ag ending to -e; its antonym is Pahl. gaǰastag < *vi-ǰasta- 'accursed', and the intensive compound far-xojaste فرخجسته (Nourai's extra form) is far/farr- + xojaste, exactly as Mo'in notes at Borhan p.1453. Mo'in's footnote in Borhan-e Qate' (p.717-718) spells out the same chain from the Avestan root ǰad-. gedā گدا 'beggar' is the more doubtful half. Mo'in (Borhan p.1776 footnote) derives it precisely as Nourai does — 'در اوستایی gad (خواهش کردن، خواستن) ... بارتولمه AiW', with Kurdish gadā — so a nomen agentis *gadā(g) 'one who asks' from the same *gʷʰedʰ- root. Modern Wiktionary, however, prefers a different Iranian etymon, comparing Avestan gaδa- 'robber' (cf. Av. *gaδōtāt- 'Räuberbande', AiW) and Pashto ġal 'thief', with a semantic path 'robber' > 'vagrant' > 'beggar'; that requires -δ- rather than -d- and is not the *gʷʰedʰ- root. Both etymologies are live; Nourai follows the older Persian (Mo'in/Bartholomae) view.",
