          "derivation": "The direction of borrowing Nourai draws — Persian gedā into Arabic — is the accepted one. C. E. Bosworth in Encyclopaedia Iranica (BEGGING i, 1989) writes that Arabic 'mokaddī (abstracts: takdīa, kodya “begging”), found in adab literature (e.g. in Jāḥeẓ, with his Ketāb ḥīal al-mokaddīn ...) from the 3rd/9th century onwards ... is clearly derived from Persian gadā “beggar, begging”' (citing Bosworth, The Mediaeval Islamic Underworld: The Banū Sāsān, Leiden 1976, I 40, II 244, and Pellat, 'Mukaddī', EI²). Arabic re-analysed the Persian noun as a hollow/defective triliteral k-d-y and built a form II verb kaddā 'to beg' with the noun kudya كُدْية 'begging' and the participle mukaddī مُكَدّي 'beggar'. The reservation is the form Nourai prints: 'kadâ' is not a lexical Arabic noun meaning 'beggar' — it stands for the verbal stem كدّى kaddā; the classical Arabic dictionaries' كُدْية kudya also carries the older concrete sense 'hard patch of ground', so the native lexicographers treated the group as Arabic. Nourai's arrow is right; his citation form is a stem, not an attested noun.",
