          "derivation": "Persian تکدّی takaddi 'begging' is the Arabic form-V verbal noun تَكَدٍّ takaddin of the denominative كدّى kaddā built on the borrowed Persian gedā — so it is a classic Persian → Arabic → Persian round trip: NP gedā > Arab. kaddā / kudya (3rd/9th c., Jāḥeẓ's Ketāb ḥiyal al-mukaddīn) > the Arabic masdar takaddī, re-borrowed into New Persian as the learned/formal word for 'begging' (Persian keeps gedā'i as the native synonym). Nourai's extra form مُتَکَدّی motakaddi is the corresponding form-V active participle 'one who begs'. Encyclopaedia Iranica heads the whole article 'BEGGING, Pers. gadāʾī, takaddī (Ar. also kodya, takdīa), soʾāl', i.e. it treats takaddī exactly as the Arabic-shaped Persian term for the same thing.",
