      "note": "The root box itself is right: Pokorny 491 has 'gʷhen- ,schwellen, strotzen, Fülle‘; gʷhono-s ,üppig, reichlich‘' with Skt. ā-ghaná-, ghaná-, Gk. euthenéō and — the Iranian witness Nourai does NOT use — 'npers. ā-gandan ,anfüllen‘, āganiš ,voll‘'; Watkins/AHD likewise: 'gʷhen-2. To swell, abound. [Pok. 1. gʷhen- 491].' What is wrong on this chart is not the root but both branches hung under it. Branch A (šan / šyaŋha / āšiyān / golšan / kāšān / Hušang) belongs to the 'dwell, settle' root *ḱey-/*tḱey-: Bartholomae AiW col. 1707 gives 'j. šayana- n. ‚Wohnstätte, Aufenthalt, Heimat‘' (Armenian loan šēn 'village'), Mo'in's own footnote in the Borhan page Nourai cites (BQT:46) derives آشیان from 'ā- + šyāna- بمعنی جای و سرای، بارتولمه ۱۷۰۷', and Wiktionary's Persian suffix -šan is 'from *tḱey- “to cultivate, settle, live”, cf. Avestan šaiiana- “place of residence”'. Nourai's own NOTE ('See Kweye for another possible root of āšīyān(eh)') points at exactly this, and modern scholarship takes that side without hesitation. Branch B (Skt khaṇḍa > qand > candy) is a real and well-documented chain word-for-word, but its head is not Indo-European at all: AHD calls khaṇḍaḥ 'piece, fragment' 'perhaps of Munda origin' and Klein 'prob. of Dravidian origin (cp. Tamil kaṇṭu “candy”)'. So the root is confirmed but neither branch actually descends from it. Page image kept at data/verification/sources/refs/ocr/BOOK/212.png.",
