          "derivation": "There is no Avestan or Old Persian word *šan; what Avestan actually has is šayana- n. 'Wohnstätte, Aufenthalt, Heimat' (Bartholomae, AiW col. 1707, with the Armenian loanword šēn 'village'), a -ana- derivative of the verb šay-/sōi- 'to lie, rest, dwell' (Gathic syā-), i.e. PIE *ḱey-/*tḱey- 'to lie down, settle' — the root Nourai himself cross-references as 'Kweye'. 'šyangh' is the second element of the Avestan proper name Haošyaŋha- (the first Pēšdādian king), which Mo'in, following Pourdavoud, glosses in the Borhan footnote as 'کسی که منازل خوب فراهم سازد' ('he who provides good dwellings'), i.e. hu- 'good' + a *šyaŋha- of the same 'dwelling' family; the second element is in fact contested (others attach it to Av. saŋh- 'to pronounce'). Either way the family belongs to *ḱey-, not to *gʷʰen- 'to swell': Pokorny p.491 lists no Iranian 'house' word under this root, and *gʷʰen- would give Iranian ǰan-/gan-, not šan-. Nourai's own NOTE ('cognate with kan 1, xan, gan 1: to dig out and mound up (a shelter)') and his cross-reference to Kweye show he was aware of the alternatives; modern scholarship takes the Kweye side. The whole branch rests on ZMA (Jonaydi, Zendegi va Mohajerat-e Aryaian), a popular, non-academic work.",
