          "derivation": "Neither Sanskrit word supports the arrow. (a) 'ğanâ : ditch' is not a Sanskrit lexeme the dictionaries know; the Sanskrit words for 'ditch, pit, mine' are khā-, khani-, khāta-, from the verb khan- 'to dig' — the family Nourai alludes to in his own note on node #1 ('kan 1, xan, gan 1: to dig out'). khan- is not from *gʷʰen- 'to swell': the *gʷʰen- reflexes in Sanskrit are ā-ghaná- 'swelling, abundant' and ghaná- 'dense, compact mass' (Pokorny 491), which is a quite different word. The printed page really does read 'ğanâ' (page image kept), so the extraction is faithful and the odd form is the book's. (b) khaṇḍa- खण्ड 'piece, fragment; a lump of candied sugar' (with the derivative khaṇḍaka-) is a real and central word — it is the true source of qand/candy — but it is not inherited Indo-European at all: AHD s.v. candy calls khaṇḍaḥ 'piece, fragment, perhaps of Munda origin', and Klein (p. 230-31 s.v. candy) says Arab. qand and 'OI. khaṇḍakaḥ “candy” is prob. of Dravidian origin. Cp. Tamil kaṇṭu “candy”, kaṭṭu “to harden, condense”'. So the words below this box are genuine, but their attachment to PIE *gʷʰen- is rejected by modern scholarship.",
